.md
### SpinShow: Automatically Animated 3D Plots
It may come in handy to automatically animate any 3D plot with a defined speed and rotation angle for presentation purposes.

> The old Graphics`Animation` package had the SpinShow[] function... –
> J. M.'s missing motivation
> / Commented Sep 28, 2012 at 11:57

Wish granted.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### SpinShow: Automatically Animated 3D Plots
It may come in handy to automatically animate any 3D plot with a defined speed and rotation angle for presentation purposes.

> The old Graphics`Animation` package had the SpinShow[] function... –
> J. M.'s missing motivation
> / Commented Sep 28, 2012 at 11:57

Wish granted.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
AnimatedKnot = Graphics3D[{MaterialShading["Bronze"], 
  KnotData["SolomonSeal", "ImageData"]}, "SpinShow"->True]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["Fae25c1a9-8814-4e41-9724-81d9f7e24225"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgUJJ6YamSYbJlrqWlgYmuiapJoY6lqaG5noWhimWKaZpxqZGBmZAgCTkBU1"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Or, if you want it to move like a boomerang, use `"SpinShow" -> angle`. The speed is controlled by the second argument in the list `"SpinShow" -> {angle, speed}`.

:::tip
The animation stops when the 3D plot is no longer visible.
:::

You can put it on one of your slides if you wish:

